{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1774293683,"nonce":"Kp188oiRttg5r0z20LfkpFAGsGY=","message":"## Additional methods from RLRMP robust control analysis\n\nTwo additional training methods have been identified that are distinct from RL/ES — they are still backprop-based but modify how losses are aggregated or how perturbations are generated:\n\n### CVaR (Conditional Value-at-Risk)\nFor each condition, sample K perturbation realizations, sort losses, backprop only through the worst (1-alpha) fraction (e.g., worst 10%). Interpolates between expected-cost (standard backprop) and worst-case optimization. Implementation is ~10 lines — just loss aggregation, no new trainer needed. Could be a flag/option on the existing TaskTrainer.\n\n### APT (Adversarial Perturbation Training)\nInner gradient ascent loop finds worst-case perturbation w (maximizing loss subject to energy constraint), then outer loop trains network parameters against that worst-case. This is the differentiable-simulator analog of H-infinity robust control. More complex — requires model-specific inner loop (gradient ascent on perturbation depends on forward dynamics).\n\nThese sit between standard backprop and RL on the complexity spectrum. CVaR fits cleanly into the existing TaskTrainer. APT may need a new trainer or a significant extension.\n\nSee rlrmp issues 84ee4ff (method details) and c05998e (Part 2.5 experiment).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.6 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","files":null}]}